  {"en":"mixed Black and white heritage (mulato)","topic":"careful","words":[{"es":"mulato, mulata","where":"heard in the Caribbean, Brazil and Colombia"}],"note":"It's usually traced to mula (mule) and comes from colonial racial categories, which is why many people find it offensive. Some people in the Caribbean use it neutrally or for themselves. Don't use it for someone else unless you know they're fine with it."},
  {"en":"Indian, Indigenous person (indio)","topic":"careful","words":[{"es":"indio, india","where":"often an insult"},{"es":"indígena","where":"safe everywhere"}],"note":"In much of Latin America, indio has been used to call someone uneducated or backward, so it can really sting. Say indígena. In the Dominican Republic it has long described skin color, and usually isn't meant as an insult."},
  {"en":"Black, as a nickname (negro, negrita)","topic":"careful","words":[{"es":"negro, negra","where":"fine for the color; nickname use varies"},{"es":"mi negra, negrito","where":"affectionate in the Caribbean and elsewhere"}],"note":"Between family and partners these can be terms of endearment, whatever the person's color. Said by a stranger or a foreigner, they can land very differently. Understand them when you hear them, and don't copy them."},
  {"en":"fat, as a nickname (gordo, gordita)","topic":"careful","words":[{"es":"gordo, gorda","where":"direct, can be rude"},{"es":"gordito, mi gorda","where":"often affectionate"}],"note":"People may call a partner or child gordita with real affection, whatever their size. That doesn't carry over to strangers, where it comments on someone's body."},
  {"en":"Asian people (chino)","topic":"careful","words":[{"es":"chino, china","where":"used across Latin America"},{"es":"asiático","where":"safe everywhere"}],"note":"People of any East Asian background often get called chino. It's not always meant badly, but many find it dismissive, so use the person's actual nationality or asiático. In Mexico, chino also means curly-haired."},
  {"en":"right now (ahorita)","topic":"careful","words":[{"es":"ahorita","where":"means different things by country"}],"note":"In Mexico it can mean right now or in a while. In the Caribbean it often means later, sometimes much later, so ask if timing matters."}
